findings.fix:
The one-line fix is correct as proposed and I would apply it. At apps/demos/src/demos/eclipse-geometry/main.ts:1241, inside the `if (runMode === "simulate")` branch, add the missing third copy so all three longitudes reach `state`:

    state.sunLonDeg = simulation.sunLonDeg;
    state.moonLonDeg = simulation.moonLonDeg;   // <-- add
    state.nodeLonDeg = simulation.nodeLonDeg;
    moonLon.value = String(Math.round(simulation.moonLonDeg));
    render();

This works because `render()` reads `state.moonLonDeg` while `isAnimating` (main.ts:484) and writes it back unchanged (main.ts:490), so seeding it before the call is sufficient. It is exactly the pattern the sibling branches already use (main.ts:1192 and main.ts:1207 both write `state.moonLonDeg` then `render()`), so it is a DRY correction, not a new mechanism. I also rounded the slider write to match main.ts:1195/1210 — `#moonLon` is `min=0 max=360` with the default step of 1, so an unrounded float string is snapped by the browser anyway and the rounded form keeps the three branches identical.

Two additions beyond the proposal:

1. Also fix the stale post-run display. The completion block at main.ts:1256-1270 calls `stopLoop()` without a following `render()`, so the last frame is always the one drawn while `runMode` was still "simulate". Add `render();` after `stopLoop();` there (and in the `stopSimulation` handler) so the idle path re-reads the slider and the readout, slider and drawing provably agree once the run ends.

2. The proposed e2e assertion would not work. "Assert that `#phaseAngle` completes more than one full cycle during a 10-year run" is unobservable: at the default medium speed the run lasts 1.83 s while the phase sweeps 123.7 cycles, so a Playwright poll (bounded by rAF, ~110 frames) cannot count cycles, and the test would be flaky on speed and machine load. Assert the direct, deterministic observable of this defect instead: select `#simSpeed` = slow, click `#runSimulation`, and poll that `#moonLonValue` changes from its pre-run text — today it provably never does for the whole run. Then, after "Simulation complete.", assert `#moonLonValue` agrees with the `#moonLon` input value, which pins the stale-display half of the bug too.

Nothing else depends on `state.moonLonDeg` staying frozen during a run: `stepSimulation` (main.ts:1130-1137) and every eclipse count read `sim.*` exclusively, so the fix cannot change any reported statistic — it only makes the drawing and the readouts match the simulation that is already being computed correctly.

Do NOT take the proposed grid-driven connected-component fix. It has three problems: `gridCache` is null on the first paint and during a slider drag with `deferGridRebuild` set (regimeMap.ts:481), so the labels would have no source exactly when the panel first appears; a flood-fill over 8000 cells would run on every render frame, including every slider move; and it puts grid-derived labels inside `drawAnalyticalOverlay`, whose job is to annotate the analytical curves, so the label could disagree with the dashed line it labels. It is also new per-frame machinery, which CLAUDE.md asks to justify with a measured need first.

Apply the cheaper fix instead: derive all three anchors from the three boundary functions already imported and already in scope in that function (`mu` and `muE` are its own parameters), with no grid, no new pass, and no per-frame cost. In `drawAnalyticalOverlay`, replace the three literal `fillText` calls (regimeMap.ts:255-262) with:

  const clampRho = (r: number) =>
    Math.min(cfg.logRhoMax - 0.5, Math.max(cfg.logRhoMin + 0.5, r));
  const GAS_REF_LOGT = 5.5, RAD_REF_LOGT = 8, DEG_REF_LOGT = 4.5;
  // gas lives between P_gas=P_rad (below it) and P_gas=P_deg (above it)
  const gasY = clampRho(0.5 * (gasRadBoundaryLogRho(GAS_REF_LOGT, mu)
                             + gasDegBoundaryLogRho(GAS_REF_LOGT, mu, muE)));
  // radiation lives below both of its boundaries
  const radY = clampRho(Math.min(gasRadBoundaryLogRho(RAD_REF_LOGT, mu),
                                 radDegBoundaryLogRho(RAD_REF_LOGT, muE)) - 3);
  // degeneracy lives above both of its boundaries
  const degY = clampRho(Math.max(gasDegBoundaryLogRho(DEG_REF_LOGT, mu, muE),
                                 radDegBoundaryLogRho(DEG_REF_LOGT, muE)) + 4.3);

then draw GAS at (GAS_REF_LOGT, gasY), RAD at (RAD_REF_LOGT, radY), DEG at (DEG_REF_LOGT, degY). The labels now move with mu and mu_e exactly as the dashed boundaries do, so they cannot disagree with the curve they annotate.

Verified against the full model at the four compositions reachable from the UI (X=0.34/Y=0.64, X=0.74/Y=0.24, X=0/Y=0.98, pure H): gasY = -2.46/-2.56/-2.35/-2.62 all classify "gas"; radY = -1.59/-1.74/-1.39/-1.82 all classify "radiation"; degY = 3.96/3.91/3.99/3.88 all classify "degeneracy".

If a one-line change is wanted first, `logRhoToY(6, ...)` -> `logRhoToY(-2.5, ...)` on regimeMap.ts:256 is correct on its own for every composition the UI allows, so it can ship immediately and the parameterized version can follow.

Two things to add alongside, since nothing currently catches this class of error: a logic-level test that, for each of gas/radiation/degeneracy, evaluates `StellarEosModel.evaluateStateCgs` at that channel's label anchor and asserts `dominantPressureChannel` equals the channel the label names (run over at least the solar-core and X=0/Y=0.98 compositions) — assert the channel, not the coordinates, or the test will just re-pin whatever is there; and separately fix the half-cell offset in `drawExactGrid` (use pitch `pa.w/(grid.xCells - 1)` with cells centred on their sample points, or sample at cell centres) so that the fill a student reads actually corresponds to the state it was computed at.

Three steps, in order.

1. Extract the challenge array out of main.ts into apps/demos/src/demos/moon-phases/logic.ts (or a new challenges.ts beside it), taking MoonPhasesModel as an injected callback the way logic.ts already does at logic.ts:27-33. This is required first: main.ts touches the DOM at module scope (main.ts:42-52), so no unit test can import the graders where they currently live.

2. Gate each quarter check on waxing/waning from @cosmic/physics ANDed with the existing fraction band, rather than hardcoding 270/90 in the demo — this keeps the phase-angle convention owned by packages/physics. For the First Quarter challenge (main.ts:853-868):

  const diff = Math.abs(illum - 0.5);
  const sense = MoonPhasesModel.waxingWaningFromPhaseAngleDeg(angleDeg); // "Waxing" at 270
  if (diff <= 0.03 && sense === "Waxing") return { correct: true, close: false, message: `$f \approx ${formatFraction(illum)}$.` };
  if (diff <= 0.03) return { correct: false, close: true, message: "That is Third Quarter — half lit, but waning. First Quarter is the waxing half, near $\\alpha = 270^{\\circ}$." };
  if (diff <= 0.08 && sense === "Waxing") return { correct: false, close: true, message: `Close: $f \approx ${formatFraction(illum)}$.` };
  return { correct: false, close: false, message: "Move toward the waxing quarter phase (50% lit)." };

and the mirror for Third Quarter (sense === "Waning", wrong-quarter message naming First Quarter and $\\alpha = 90^{\\circ}$). Note the waxing gate alone is insufficient (it accepts alpha = 200, f = 0.030), so keep the fraction band as the conjunct. Giving the opposite quarter its own `close` message turns the current false positive into the teaching moment the challenge was for, instead of a bare rejection.

3. Add assertions to logic.test.ts that encode the discrimination, not just the pass case: the First Quarter check REJECTS alpha = 90 (and returns the Third-Quarter-naming message), ACCEPTS alpha = 270, rejects alpha = 200 despite "Waxing"; and the mirror three for Third Quarter. The rejection assertions are the ones that would have caught this — a test that only asserts 270 passes is green against the current broken code.

Also fix the hint/grader asymmetry while in there: both hints are already correct under the repo convention (270 = First Quarter, 90 = Third Quarter per logic.ts:16-21 and CLAUDE.md), so no hint change is needed — only the graders are wrong.

The finder's core fix is right; it needs two corrections and does not close everything it claims.

1. Add one conversion in apps/demos/src/demos/parallax-distance/main.ts and route every physical->screen mapping in the orbit panel through it:

   function physToScreen(center: Vec2, vecPhys: Vec2, scalePx: number): Vec2 {
     return { x: center.x + vecPhys.x * scalePx, y: center.y - vecPhys.y * scalePx };
   }

   Use it at main.ts:703 for starPos (restores (280, 72) px, matching index.html:167/173) and in orbitToScreen (main.ts:486-489), which makes phase 0 -> (418, 220), phase 45 -> (377.6, 122.4), phase 90 -> (280, 82): counter-clockwise, as CLAUDE.md requires. Applying it to the axis endpoints (main.ts:704-705) is harmless but not load-bearing, since axisHat.y = 0.

2. Do NOT reposition targetDirectionLabel from the computed starPos, as the finder proposes. Once the flip lands, the authored (280, 58) is already 14 px above the restored star, and setLabel would center "Target direction" at (280, 66) — 8 px from "Target star" at (280, 66) — because setLabel overwrites x with point.x and every stage label is `text-anchor: middle` (style.css:233). Instead leave the direction label authored (or place it at the line's midpoint, around (280, 140), away from both the Sun and the star) and give targetStarLabel an x offset so it stops sitting on its own dot and on the direction line: add a dx argument to setLabel (main.ts:517-520) and call it with dx = +28, dy = -6, reproducing the authored x=306 (index.html:174).

3. The flip alone does not fix the Earth-touches-the-star geometry: with STAR_RADIUS_PX = 148 and ORBIT_RADIUS_PX = 138 the Earth dot (r=10) and star dot (r=8) still overlap once per orbit (at phase 270 after the flip) and the Earth->star ray degenerates to zero length. Raise STAR_RADIUS_PX so the star clears the orbit path by more than the two radii (about 185 px puts it at y = 35, inside the viewBox and clear of the geometryCue at y=28, or draw it clipped at the panel edge with a break symbol to signal "much farther"). Propose the number to Anna rather than picking it silently, since it is a pedagogical scale choice.

4. Add the regression test that is currently missing: assert the rendered attribute, not the basis. In main.integration.test.ts, after render(), expect targetStar's cy to equal "72.00" and expect earthNow's cy at phase 90 to be less than ORBIT_CENTER.y (i.e. the orbit goes up first). logic.test.ts:43-49 passes under both the correct and the broken renderer and is not evidence.

5. Re-check the left/right sense while in here, separately from this fix: detectorToScreen (main.ts:492-502) yields a detector x offset proportional to +earthPosAu.x, the same sign as Earth's screen x in the orbit panel, so the apparent stellar shift tracks the observer's displacement rather than opposing it. The y-flip neither creates nor cures that; it needs its own decision about which on-sky direction maps to screen right in the detector panel, and should be raised with Anna as a convention question before any sign is changed.

In apps/demos/src/demos/planetary-conjunctions/main.ts, schedule the count on the analytic alignment time instead of the threshold edge. Alignments are exactly at t = k*P_syn here (verified: separation at k*P_syn is 0 to 1e-13 deg for all four presets), so replace main.ts:203-212 with:

  const nextAlignmentDay = (conjunctionCount + 1) * synPeriodDays;
  if (Number.isFinite(nextAlignmentDay) && elapsedDays >= nextAlignmentDay) {
    conjunctionCount++;
    flashOpacity = 0.8;
    setLiveRegionText(
      status,
      `${alignmentLabel(currentAlignment())} ${conjunctionCount} at day ${Math.round(nextAlignmentDay)}.`
    );
  }

Announce `nextAlignmentDay`, not `elapsedDays`, so the day is exact at any speed rather than quantized by the frame step (at speed 100 the step is 1.667 d). Keep `inConj` and CONJUNCTION_THRESHOLD_DEG for the alignment ray only (main.ts:215-227) and leave the separation readout untouched. `lastWasConjunction` and the `elapsedDays > 1` guard both become dead - remove the declaration (main.ts:119) and the reset (main.ts:294).

Two things this fixes for free, which the finder's variants do not: the day-0 trap disappears, because event 1 now lands at exactly 779.9 d, so a student anchoring on the visible startup ray measures P_syn correctly; and the flash fires at the alignment rather than ~10.8 d early or ~10.8 d late.

Two follow-ups worth a line each, not part of this fix: (a) add a comment that the analytic t = k*P_syn shortcut holds only while orbits are circular - model_notes already declares eccentricity ignored, and if eccentric orbits ever land, the signed-wrap-plus-interpolation detector is the right structure to switch to; (b) the announcement uses `Math.round(day)` while the Days-elapsed readout uses `formatDays`, which switches to years above 1000 d (logic.ts:196-200), so event 2 is announced as "day 1549" while the readout reads "4.2 yr" - make both use `formatDays` for consistency.

Keep one source of truth for the field and make the separation framing a floor that zoom cannot cross, then prove the slider still does something.

1. In `apps/demos/src/demos/telescope-resolution/logic.ts`, delete `zoomedFov` and replace `computeFovArcsec` with a single function that takes the zoom:

```ts
export const FOV_MIN_ARCSEC = 0.4;
export const FOV_MAX_ARCSEC = 500;
/** Field must always contain the pair with margin: 1.2 * separation. */
export function computeFovArcsec(args: {
  thetaEffArcsec: number;
  separationArcsec: number;
  zoom: number;
}): number {
  const { thetaEffArcsec, separationArcsec } = args;
  const zoom = clamp(args.zoom, 1, 20);
  const framingFloor = separationArcsec > 0 ? 1.2 * separationArcsec : 6 * thetaEffArcsec / zoom;
  const magnified = Math.max(6 * thetaEffArcsec, 3 * separationArcsec) / zoom;
  return clamp(Math.max(magnified, framingFloor), FOV_MIN_ARCSEC, FOV_MAX_ARCSEC);
}
```
Note `magnified` divides the whole base field (not just the diffraction term), so zoom stays live from 1x down to the floor instead of dying at 1x: for the Hubble default it runs 3.000 -> 1.200 arcsec over zoom 1 -> 2.5 and then clamps. Collapse main.ts:374-376 to one call.

2. Because the field now saturates, the saturation must be visible rather than mysterious. When `Math.max(magnified, framingFloor)` returns the floor, append to the FOV label that the field is held to keep both stars framed — e.g. `FOV: 1.20 arcsec (6x, field held to frame the pair)` — so a student dragging the slider with an unchanging picture reads the demo's actual lesson ("more magnification, no more detail") instead of a broken control. That turns the clamp into the teaching moment the exhibit's first learning goal and named misconception are asking for.

3. The separation floor should also be the real reason the pair is drawn, so prefer growing the PSF in pixels over narrowing the field: once the floor is hit, keep rendering at the floor field and let the gamma/stretch carry the "bigger but no sharper" story.

4. Tests in `logic.test.ts` — the current 14 assertions on `computeFovArcsec` and `zoomedFov` all test one function in isolation on synthetic numbers and never compose them, which is why this survived. Add, importing `telescopePresets` and `wavelengthBands` from `@cosmic/data-telescopes` and `TelescopeResolutionModel` from `@cosmic/physics`:
   (a) framing: for every preset x every band x zoom in 1..20 x separation in {0.001, 0.1, 1, 10, 1000}, assert `fov / 2 > separationArcsec / 2` strictly, with the margin — this is the invariant that was never asserted;
   (b) non-inertness: assert the field strictly decreases with zoom until it reaches the floor, so a fix that freezes the slider fails;
   (c) a regression case pinned to real numbers: Hubble (2.4 m) in the visible band at separation 1 arcsec and zoom 6 must return a field of at least 1.2 arcsec, where today it returns 0.5 arcsec.

5. Separately, consider whether `status: draft` / `readiness: experimental` is where this demo should stay until the zoom control is reworked. It is correctly flagged today, so no readiness change is owed — but `content_verified: true` in the frontmatter deserves a second look given that the primary stage renders empty for 10 of 12 presets.
